Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Severe vaginal dryness, Dyspareunia, Pale/inelastic vaginal mucosa, Elevated vaginal pH >5.0

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower urogenital pain, intractable dyspareunia, and debilitating dysuria mimicking a severe urinary tract infection, requiring immediate relief.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, burning vulvovaginal pain, microscopic bleeding, and intense dysuria causing significant distress and inability to void comfortably. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively differentiate between a severe acute bacterial urinary tract infection and an extreme exacerbation of urogenital atrophy, and to initiate targeted, immediate topical symptom relief to prevent further mucosal tearing.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like raw sandpaper down there, urinating is agonizing, and any contact causes bleeding and intense burning."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive worsening of severe vaginal dryness and an absolute lack of physiological lubrication over the past 2 years following menopause. Tonight, following an attempt at intimacy, they experienced severe dyspareunia resulting in intense pain, localized burning, and the observation of small amounts of blood. They currently report extreme pruritus, burning, and intense irritation of the vulva. Dysuria is severe, accompanied by significantly increased urinary frequency, mimicking a recurrent urinary tract infection. The patient notes a history of recurrent genuine culture-positive urinary tract infections over the past year.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted in movement and sitting posture due to severe vulval hyperalgesia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe psychological distress and avoidance of intimacy due to pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, distressed by the acute pain and recent bleeding, sitting cautiously on the edge of the chair.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: External inspection reveals retraction of the labia minora. Speculum examination is deferred due to extreme pain, but direct visualization demonstrates pale, thin, and entirely inelastic vaginal mucosa devoid of normal rugae. Petechiae and easily friable mucosal surfaces are noted, which bleed upon minimal contact with a cotton swab. A small urethral caruncle is visible at the meatus. Point-of-care urinalysis is negative for nitrites and leukocytes, confirming the dysuria is due to localized mucosal atrophy rather than active cystitis. Expected investigation results would show an elevated vaginal pH typically greater than 5.0.
Environmental Observations: Over-the-counter thrush treatments visible, which the patient has used repeatedly without success.
Medication Reconciliation: No current hormonal therapies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Cystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely negative urinalysis; the dysuria is secondary to urine passing over severely atrophied, micro-fissured urethral tissue.

Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of characteristic thick white discharge and the failure of multiple over-the-counter antifungal treatments.

Lichen Sclerosus - Ruled out/unlikely due to the specific lack of the classic "figure of eight" white plaques and the direct correlation with systemic estrogen withdrawal.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing an acute, severe exacerbation of Genitourinary Syndrome of Menopause, presenting with micro-trauma, bleeding, and intense dysuria mimicking an infection. The extreme mucosal friability and pain required urgent after-hours physical evaluation to rule out an acute, complicated urinary tract infection and to immediately cease the harmful application of over-the-counter antifungal chemicals. Deferring this assessment would have resulted in continued inappropriate self-treatment, worsening mucosal damage, and prolonged, severe localized pain. Immediate diagnosis and the initiation of targeted, soothing topical therapy were medically mandated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed immediate cessation of all over-the-counter antifungal or antibacterial creams. Provided an inert, soothing barrier ointment (e.g., pure white soft paraffin) for immediate application to the vulva to protect the micro-fissures from urine. Wrote a prescription for topical vaginal estrogen cream (estriol 1mg/g), to commence daily application once the acute micro-trauma settles in 48 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: To promote tissue healing and prevent further trauma, the patient is advised to apply a thick layer of the prescribed barrier ointment before every act of urination. Preventing the sharp, burning pain of urine hitting raw tissue will provide immediate, powerful positive reinforcement, ensuring they build the habit of consistently protecting the mucosal barrier.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 2 weeks to assess the response to the topical estrogen therapy and review the need for long-term maintenance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review is required if the patient develops a high fever, cloudy or foul-smelling urine, or if the minor spotting transitions into heavy vaginal bleeding.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The thinning of the tissues is a natural consequence of menopause, but the severe symptoms require active, ongoing medical management.
Clinical Course: The burning and pain should improve significantly within 3-4 days of using the barrier cream. The topical estrogen will take several weeks to fully rebuild the thickness and elasticity of the tissues.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop a fever, if your urine becomes cloudy, or if you experience heavy bleeding rather than just a few drops, you must seek immediate medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Without estrogen, the skin in the urogenital area has become extremely thin, dry, and fragile, causing it to crack and bleed easily. The barrier ointment will act as a shield against stinging urine, while the prescription estrogen cream will specifically deliver the missing hormone back into the tissue to make it thick and robust again.
Actionable Steps: Stop using all thrush creams immediately as they are burning the raw skin. Apply the barrier ointment before urinating. Start the estrogen cream in 48 hours exactly as prescribed.

